WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.170
Network segmentation is the act of physically
and/or logically breaking up a network into

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.430
smaller networks.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.140
Why break up a network?

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:11.759
Physical location plays a role in this as,
in a multi-building campus, for example, it

00:00:11.759 --> 00:00:14.799
makes sense to have a local network in each
physical building.

00:00:14.799 --> 00:00:18.600
There are situations in which, though, in
a single building, you may want to break up

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:19.810
a network logically.

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:24.269
Take, for example, a computer training center
that has two classrooms, an administrative

00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:27.110
staff, and a repair shop in the back of the
building.

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:30.500
Does it make sense to have everyone on the
same network?

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:34.110
One very important aspect to think through
here is that of permissions.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:38.160
Do we want classroom students seeing what
is going on over on the administrative side

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.160
of the building?

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:40.610
Probably not.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:43.640
And the repair shop takes malware off devices.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:46.300
We definitely want that area to be isolated.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:51.051
Right there, that's three logical networks
we would want: one for the classrooms, one

00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:54.410
for the administrative staff, and one for
the repair shop.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:59.310
Again, if you think one group should be isolated
from others, that defines a network segment.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:04.320
And, as covered elsewhere in this course,
a physical network can easily be broken up

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.570
into multiple VLANs to create multiple logical
networks.

